{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SVz58ngnpMvJTnb47c3KkC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context and propose tasks for release 05.0.\n\nRelease 05.0 contains: echo, tac, nl, fold — \"text stream transforms\"\n\nI need to find the PRDs for these utilities. Let me check what's available in the project context.\n\nThe constraints say I can only propose 1 task max, and it must be for release 05.0.\n\nLet me look at what PRDs exist. The analysis shows rel05.0 use cases:\n- rel05.0-uc001-echo\n- rel05.0-uc002-tac\n- rel05.0-uc003-nl\n\n\n- rel05.0-uc004-fold\n\nAll have specstatus: spec_complete, codestatus: not started.\n\nI need to read the PRDs to anchor tasks to specific requirement IDs, but the constraints prevent me from exploring the filesystem or reading files. The project context only shows that these PRDs exist without their actual content, so I can't reference specific requirement IDs to ground my task proposals.\n\nLooking at the naming pattern from what I do know—prd001-testutils, prd002-sys, prd003-format, prd008-ls, prd009-du—I can infer that echo, tac, nl, and fold would have their own PRD numbers in the sequence. Since I can't access the actual PRD files to determine their exact numbers or requirement IDs, I'll need to work with the likely file paths and reference them by their feature names instead.\n\nEcho seems like the most foundational utility to start with—it's the simplest text stream operation and would logically come first in the implementation order. I'll propose a task anchored to what would likely be the echo PRD, even though I can't cite the exact requirement ID without seeing the actual specification. I'm considering whether to combine echo with other simple utilities like tac or fold since echo's implementation is so minimal, but I need to balance that against the constraint of keeping tasks under 250 lines and respecting the separat